window.__SHB_EGYPTIAN_BOOK_QUESTIONS__=(window.__SHB_EGYPTIAN_BOOK_QUESTIONS__||[]).concat([{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q1","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The term growth refers to","o":["Increase in cell size and function","Functional maturity","Increase in cell size","Increase in intracellular substance"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Growth is a quantitative increase in body size, usually assessed by serial measurements such as weight, length/height and head circumference. The course document explicitly defines growth as an increase in size.","more":"Growth is a quantitative increase in body size, usually assessed by serial measurements such as weight, length/height and head circumference. The course document explicitly defines growth as an increase in size.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.3; anthropometric measurements are listed from p.4.\n\nHigh-yield point: Growth = size; development = acquisition of function and skills.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q1 — Meaning of growth","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 4 · صفحة مطبوعة 1 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 1.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q2","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The term development refers to","o":["Increase in cell size and function","Functional maturity","Increase in cell size","Increase in intracellular substance"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Development is progressive acquisition of skills, function, and adaptive abilities as organ systems mature. It is not simply an increase in physical size.","more":"Development is progressive acquisition of skills, function, and adaptive abilities as organ systems mature. It is not simply an increase in physical size.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Developmental Assessment—red flags, pp.1–2; Developmental-assessment, p.2.\n\nHigh-yield point: Assess development across gross motor, fine motor/vision, hearing-speech-language, and social domains.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q2 — Meaning of development","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 4 · صفحة مطبوعة 1 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 2.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q4","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The process of growth ends at","o":["Five years","Fully mature adult","Birth","14 years"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Somatic growth continues through childhood and puberty until adult physical maturity and epiphyseal closure. The wording “fully mature adult” is the best of the listed choices; specific tissues may mature at different times.","more":"Somatic growth continues through childhood and puberty until adult physical maturity and epiphyseal closure. The wording “fully mature adult” is the best of the listed choices; specific tissues may mature at different times.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.3 (fetal, infantile, childhood and pubertal phases).\n\nHigh-yield point: Growth velocity falls after puberty; linear growth stops when growth plates fuse.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q4 — When growth ends","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 4 · صفحة مطبوعة 1 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 4.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q5","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"The following is not commonly used measurement for the assessment of growth","o":["Mid arm circumference","Weight","Height","Head circumference"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Core serial anthropometry includes weight, length/height, and head circumference in young children; mid-upper-arm circumference is also a recognized anthropometric measure, particularly for assessing acute malnutrition. The stem says “not commonly used measurement for assessment of growth”, so the source key selecting mid-arm circumference is questionable depending on whether the question means general growth charting or nutritional assessment. The syllabus includes anthropometric measurements and mid-arm circumference as a nutritional measure; despite that, the intended general-growth-chart answer is often MUAC.","more":"Core serial anthropometry includes weight, length/height, and head circumference in young children; mid-upper-arm circumference is also a recognized anthropometric measure, particularly for assessing acute malnutrition. The stem says “not commonly used measurement for assessment of growth”, so the source key selecting mid-arm circumference is questionable depending on whether the question means general growth charting or nutritional assessment. The syllabus includes anthropometric measurements and mid-arm circumference as a nutritional measure; despite that, the intended general-growth-chart answer is often MUAC.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.4 (anthropometric measurements); p.6 (mid-arm circumference and skinfold measurements).\n\nHigh-yield point: MUAC is valid anthropometry, especially for malnutrition screening; it is simply less routinely plotted as a general growth-chart parameter than weight/length/OFC.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q5 — Common measurements used to assess growth","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 4 · صفحة مطبوعة 1 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 5.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q8","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The growth in female differs from male","o":["Slower","Late growth spurt","Faster","Earlier growth spurt"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"On average, girls enter puberty and experience the pubertal growth spurt earlier than boys. This is a population average, not an absolute rule for every child.","more":"On average, girls enter puberty and experience the pubertal growth spurt earlier than boys. This is a population average, not an absolute rule for every child.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.3 (pubertal growth phase); Endocrine 2025, pp.2 and 49 (puberty).\n\nHigh-yield point: Girls generally reach peak height velocity earlier; boys usually have a later and often larger peak.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q8 — Sex difference in timing of growth spurt","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 5 · صفحة مطبوعة 2 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 8.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q9","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Emotionalfactorinfluencesthegrowthby","o":["Disturbed neurochemicalregulation of growth hormone","Decreasingthyroidstimulatinghormone","Increasing growth hormone","All of the above"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Severe psychosocial stress or emotional deprivation can impair growth through neuroendocrine mechanisms, including altered hypothalamic-pituitary growth-hormone regulation. “All of the above” is not supported because the specific directions given for TSH and GH are not universally true.","more":"Severe psychosocial stress or emotional deprivation can impair growth through neuroendocrine mechanisms, including altered hypothalamic-pituitary growth-hormone regulation. “All of the above” is not supported because the specific directions given for TSH and GH are not universally true.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.3 (childhood growth and profound chronic unhappiness/decreased GH secretion); Endocrine 2025, p.47 (psychological/deprivation causes of growth failure).\n\nHigh-yield point: Consider psychosocial deprivation in a child with poor growth after assessing nutrition and chronic disease.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q9 — Effect of emotional factors on growth","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 5 · صفحة مطبوعة 2 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 9.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q10","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Lineargrowthoccursfastin","o":["Infancy","Adolescence","School period","Infancyandadolescence"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Linear growth is particularly rapid during infancy and again during the pubertal growth spurt; childhood between these phases is more steady.","more":"Linear growth is particularly rapid during infancy and again during the pubertal growth spurt; childhood between these phases is more steady.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.3 (growth phases).\n\nHigh-yield point: Monitor velocity over time, not a single height measurement.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q10 — Periods of fastest linear growth","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 5 · صفحة مطبوعة 2 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 10.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q11","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The brain grows faster at","o":["Adolescence","Toddler","Infancy","Preschool"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"The brain undergoes very rapid growth in infancy, with major early expansion and maturation. Brain development continues beyond infancy, but the largest early growth velocity is in the first years.","more":"The brain undergoes very rapid growth in infancy, with major early expansion and maturation. Brain development continues beyond infancy, but the largest early growth velocity is in the first years.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Developmental Assessment—red flags, pp.1–2; Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.3–5 (early growth monitoring).\n\nHigh-yield point: Head circumference is an indirect clinical measure of brain growth in infancy.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q11 — Period of fastest brain growth","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 5 · صفحة مطبوعة 2 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 11.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q16","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Neonatewordisreferredtothefollowingperiodof postnatal life","o":["First four weeks","First two weeks","First four months","First six months"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The neonatal period is the first 28 completed days (four weeks) after birth.","more":"The neonatal period is the first 28 completed days (four weeks) after birth.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Neonatology 2024–2025, introductory section; standard neonatal definition.\n\nHigh-yield point: Neonatal = birth through day 27; “infant” is a broader age category.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q16 — Definition of the neonatal period","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 6 · صفحة مطبوعة 3 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 16.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q18","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Growth chart de notes","o":["Rangeofbodymeasurementsatvariousages","Rangeofdevelopmentalmilestoneatvariousage","Rangeofmeasurementsofvariousparameters","None of the above"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"A growth chart displays a reference distribution or centile/standard-deviation curves for body measurements across age (and usually sex), allowing an individual child’s measurements and trajectory to be compared with a reference population.","more":"A growth chart displays a reference distribution or centile/standard-deviation curves for body measurements across age (and usually sex), allowing an individual child’s measurements and trajectory to be compared with a reference population.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.3 (growth monitoring and growth charts); p.4 (anthropometry).\n\nHigh-yield point: Interpret the trajectory and growth velocity, not an isolated centile.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q18 — What a growth chart represents","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 6 · صفحة مطبوعة 3 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 18.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q19","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Prevention","q":"Growth chart helps in community in","o":["Early diagnosis of deficiency disease","Early treatment of deficiency disease","Early diagnosis of malnutrition","Early treatment of malnutrition"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Serial growth-chart plotting can identify faltering growth and malnutrition early, prompting assessment of feeding, illness, and social factors. It is a screening/monitoring tool, not treatment itself.","more":"Serial growth-chart plotting can identify faltering growth and malnutrition early, prompting assessment of feeding, illness, and social factors. It is a screening/monitoring tool, not treatment itself.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.3 (regular monitoring and growth charts); p.2 (FTT and malnutrition).\n\nHigh-yield point: A downward crossing of centile channels merits assessment even when a single measurement is not extremely low.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q19 — Community use of growth charts","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 6 · صفحة مطبوعة 3 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 19.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q26","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Velocity of growth means","o":["Rate of growth","Rate of growth over unit time","Rate of grow that1 year","Rate of grow that5 year"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Growth velocity is the amount of growth achieved per unit of time, usually expressed as cm/year for height or g/day for weight in the relevant age group.","more":"Growth velocity is the amount of growth achieved per unit of time, usually expressed as cm/year for height or g/day for weight in the relevant age group.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.3 (growth phases and growth monitoring); Endocrine 2025, p.7 (growth failure assessment).\n\nHigh-yield point: Height velocity is often more informative than a single height centile when evaluating short stature.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q26 — Definition of growth velocity","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 7 · صفحة مطبوعة 4 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 26.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q28","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Boneagecorrespondsto","o":["Appearance soft he ossification centre","Proximalendofthetibia","Proximal end of the tal us","Distalendoftibia"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Bone age estimates skeletal maturation by assessing the appearance and maturation of ossification centres, commonly on a left hand/wrist radiograph.","more":"Bone age estimates skeletal maturation by assessing the appearance and maturation of ossification centres, commonly on a left hand/wrist radiograph.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Endocrine 2025, p.7 (left hand/wrist X-ray for bone age); pp.44 and 47 (skeletal maturation).\n\nHigh-yield point: Bone age may be delayed in constitutional delay, hypothyroidism, GH deficiency and chronic illness; it may be advanced in some conditions.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q28 — What bone age reflects","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 7 · صفحة مطبوعة 4 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 28.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q32","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The first deciduous tooth appears","o":["3-4 months","4-5 months","8-9 months","5-7 months"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"The first primary tooth usually erupts at around six months, with normal variation; a 5–7-month range is a reasonable approximate choice.","more":"The first primary tooth usually erupts at around six months, with normal variation; a 5–7-month range is a reasonable approximate choice.\n\nCurriculum alignment: GIT 2024–2025 and GIT 2025, p.4 (deciduous tooth eruption table); growth/development material.\n\nHigh-yield point: Delayed eruption is usually considered when no tooth has erupted by around 13 months in the cited GIT notes; assess context rather than one rigid date.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q32 — Age of first deciduous tooth eruption","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 7 · صفحة مطبوعة 4 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 32.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q36","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Lastprimarytoothwillbe","o":["1 st molar","2 nd pre molar","2 nd molar","1 st pre molar"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"The second primary molars are generally the last deciduous teeth to erupt, often around 23–33 months.","more":"The second primary molars are generally the last deciduous teeth to erupt, often around 23–33 months.\n\nCurriculum alignment: GIT 2024–2025 and GIT 2025, p.4 (primary tooth eruption sequence).\n\nHigh-yield point: Primary dentition totals 20 teeth.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q36 — Last primary tooth to erupt","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 7 · صفحة مطبوعة 4 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 36.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q38","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"First permanent to other ru pts at","o":["3 years","4 years","5 years","6 years"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"The first permanent molar usually erupts at approximately six years, distal to the second primary molar.","more":"The first permanent molar usually erupts at approximately six years, distal to the second primary molar.\n\nCurriculum alignment: GIT 2024–2025 and GIT 2025, p.5.\n\nHigh-yield point: The first permanent molar is often called the “six-year molar.”","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q38 — Age of first permanent tooth eruption","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 7 · صفحة مطبوعة 4 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 38.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q39","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"First permanent tooth to err up tis","o":["1 s molar","2 nd molar","1 s pre molar","2 nd pre molar"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The first permanent tooth to erupt is typically the first permanent molar, usually around age six.","more":"The first permanent tooth to erupt is typically the first permanent molar, usually around age six.\n\nCurriculum alignment: GIT 2024–2025 and GIT 2025, p.5.\n\nHigh-yield point: It erupts behind the primary second molar and does not replace a primary tooth.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q39 — Identity of the first permanent tooth","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 7 · صفحة مطبوعة 4 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 39.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q41","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The average weight of the newborn","o":["3 kgs","2 kgs","4 kgs","3.5 kgs"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"A typical term newborn weighs approximately 3–3.5 kg; 3 kg is the best single answer among the choices. Actual normal birth weight varies with gestational age, sex, and maternal/fetal factors.","more":"A typical term newborn weighs approximately 3–3.5 kg; 3 kg is the best single answer among the choices. Actual normal birth weight varies with gestational age, sex, and maternal/fetal factors.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.4 (anthropometry and birth measurements).\n\nHigh-yield point: Interpret birth weight by gestational age and centiles, not by a single population average.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q41 — Average birth weight","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 7 · صفحة مطبوعة 4 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 41.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q43","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The average weight gain in the first6 months","o":["1 kg","2 kgs","3 kgs","4 kgs"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"In early infancy, weight gain is rapid; many standard teaching schemes describe birth weight as approximately doubled by 4–6 months. A cumulative gain of about 4 kg by six months is a conventional approximate teaching figure, but individual growth depends on birth weight and feeding.","more":"In early infancy, weight gain is rapid; many standard teaching schemes describe birth weight as approximately doubled by 4–6 months. A cumulative gain of about 4 kg by six months is a conventional approximate teaching figure, but individual growth depends on birth weight and feeding.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.3–5 (infantile growth and anthropometry).\n\nHigh-yield point: Track an infant’s actual serial weight on an age- and sex-appropriate chart rather than using a fixed gain alone.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q43 — Average weight gain in the first six months","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 7 · صفحة مطبوعة 4 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 43.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q44","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The average weight gain in the first year","o":["7 kgs","6 kgs","5 kgs","4 kgs"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"The source key gives a cumulative gain of about 6 kg in the first year, consistent with a typical term newborn increasing from roughly 3–3.5 kg to around 9–10 kg by 12 months. The exact increase varies by starting weight and growth pattern.","more":"The source key gives a cumulative gain of about 6 kg in the first year, consistent with a typical term newborn increasing from roughly 3–3.5 kg to around 9–10 kg by 12 months. The exact increase varies by starting weight and growth pattern.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.3–5 (infant growth and weight monitoring).\n\nHigh-yield point: Birth weight commonly doubles by about 4–6 months and triples by about 12 months.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q44 — Average weight gain in the first year","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 8 · صفحة مطبوعة 5 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 44.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q45","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The faster weight gain is seen at","o":["First year and puberty","Second year and puberty","Puberty","Infancy"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Weight gain is fastest during infancy and increases again during the pubertal growth spurt.","more":"Weight gain is fastest during infancy and increases again during the pubertal growth spurt.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.3 (infantile and pubertal phases).\n\nHigh-yield point: Weight, length and head circumference have different velocity patterns; assess each separately.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q45 — Periods of fastest weight gain","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 8 · صفحة مطبوعة 5 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 45.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q46","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Thebirthweightwillquadrupleby","o":["20 months","24 months","36 months","40 months"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"A conventional pediatric growth rule is that birth weight doubles by about 4–6 months, triples by 12 months, and quadruples by approximately two years.","more":"A conventional pediatric growth rule is that birth weight doubles by about 4–6 months, triples by 12 months, and quadruples by approximately two years.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.4 (birth weight doubling/tripling/quadrupling milestones in the course material).\n\nHigh-yield point: Use these as approximate milestones; growth charts and clinical context take precedence.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q46 — Age by which birth weight quadruples","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 8 · صفحة مطبوعة 5 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 46.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q47","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The average length of the newborn","o":["48 cms","52 cms","50 cms","55 cms"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Average term newborn length is approximately 50 cm, with normal variation.","more":"Average term newborn length is approximately 50 cm, with normal variation.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.5 (length/height at birth about 50 cm).\n\nHigh-yield point: Measure recumbent length in children under two years using an infantometer when possible.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q47 — Average newborn length","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 8 · صفحة مطبوعة 5 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 47.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q48","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Thebirthheightwilldoubleby","o":["3 years","7 years","4 years","6 years"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"A birth length of approximately 50 cm reaches about 100 cm at around four years, so birth length roughly doubles by age four.","more":"A birth length of approximately 50 cm reaches about 100 cm at around four years, so birth length roughly doubles by age four.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.5 (50 cm at birth and about 100 cm at four years).\n\nHigh-yield point: A common rule: length 50 cm at birth, 75 cm at 1 year, and 100 cm at 4 years.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q48 — Age when birth length doubles","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 8 · صفحة مطبوعة 5 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 48.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q52","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Upper segment indicates","o":["Crown to pubic symphysis","Crowntoxiphisternum","Crown to heel","Public symphysis to heel"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The upper segment is the sitting height, measured from the crown of the head to the pubic symphysis (the source option says “pubic”, while OCR has “public” elsewhere). In modern measurement, sitting height is commonly obtained with a sitting-height stadiometer.","more":"The upper segment is the sitting height, measured from the crown of the head to the pubic symphysis (the source option says “pubic”, while OCR has “public” elsewhere). In modern measurement, sitting height is commonly obtained with a sitting-height stadiometer.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Endocrine 2025, p.44 (upper segment = sitting height; lower segment from ASIS to floor; upper segment derived from total height minus lower segment).\n\nHigh-yield point: Use consistent anatomical landmarks and technique when calculating the upper-to-lower segment ratio.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q52 — Definition of upper body segment","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 8 · صفحة مطبوعة 5 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 52.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q53","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Lower segment indicates","o":["Crown to pubic symphysis","Pubic symphysis to heel","Hipjointtoanklejoint","Xiphisternumtoheel"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"The lower segment is measured from the pubic symphysis to the floor/heel in the traditional teaching method; the Endocrine lecture describes lower segment as ASIS to floor, so the exact landmark differs slightly by convention. The source option b is the closest available.","more":"The lower segment is measured from the pubic symphysis to the floor/heel in the traditional teaching method; the Endocrine lecture describes lower segment as ASIS to floor, so the exact landmark differs slightly by convention. The source option b is the closest available.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Endocrine 2025, p.44 (lower segment and upper/lower segment ratio).\n\nHigh-yield point: Because landmarks differ between teaching sources, document the method used and do not compare ratios obtained by different techniques.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q53 — Definition of lower body segment","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 9 · صفحة مطبوعة 6 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 53.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q54","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Upper segment and lower segment become equal by","o":["17 years","12 years","10 years","8 years"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"The upper-to-lower segment ratio decreases as the legs grow proportionally faster through childhood; equality is reached around eight years in the source’s teaching scheme. Published reference values and exact age can vary with population and method.","more":"The upper-to-lower segment ratio decreases as the legs grow proportionally faster through childhood; equality is reached around eight years in the source’s teaching scheme. Published reference values and exact age can vary with population and method.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Endocrine 2025, p.44 (U/L ratio at birth, 3 years, 7–10 years and puberty).\n\nHigh-yield point: Interpret segment ratios using age-specific reference values and consistent measurements.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q54 — Age when upper and lower segments become equal","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 9 · صفحة مطبوعة 6 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 54.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q58","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The normal head circumference at birth is","o":["33-35 cms","30-33 cms","35-38 cms","38-40 cms"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Mean term newborn head circumference is about 35 cm, with a usual range around 33–37 cm. The source option 33–35 cm is narrower than many reference ranges but lies around the lower-to-middle part of normal and is the source key.","more":"Mean term newborn head circumference is about 35 cm, with a usual range around 33–37 cm. The source option 33–35 cm is narrower than many reference ranges but lies around the lower-to-middle part of normal and is the source key.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.5 (OFC at birth 35 ± 2 cm); standard newborn anthropometry.\n\nHigh-yield point: Use sex-, gestational-age- and population-specific charts; a single range is not a universal definition.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q58 — Normal head circumference at birth","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 10 · صفحة مطبوعة 7 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 58.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q59","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The head circumference at1 year","o":["45-47 cms","40-43 cms","50-53 cms","46-50 cms"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Head circumference at one year is approximately 46–47 cm on many reference schemes; the course notes approximately 47 cm.","more":"Head circumference at one year is approximately 46–47 cm on many reference schemes; the course notes approximately 47 cm.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.5 (OFC at one year about 47 cm).\n\nHigh-yield point: The head grows rapidly in the first year, so serial OFC is particularly important in infancy.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q59 — Head circumference at one year","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 10 · صفحة مطبوعة 7 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 59.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q61","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Anterior fontanelle usually closes by","o":["12 months","15 months","18 months","24 months"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"The anterior fontanel usually closes sometime between approximately 9 and 18 months; closure by 18 months is a commonly taught upper limit, with normal individual variation.","more":"The anterior fontanel usually closes sometime between approximately 9 and 18 months; closure by 18 months is a commonly taught upper limit, with normal individual variation.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Developmental and infant examination topics in the pediatric curriculum; Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.4–5 (infant anthropometry; use with clinical examination).\n\nHigh-yield point: An unusually early or late fontanel closure must be interpreted with head growth, skull shape and development.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q61 — Usual age of anterior fontanel closure","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 10 · صفحة مطبوعة 7 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 61.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q63","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Premature fusion of the skull bones is called","o":["Cr a nio syn as to s is","Microcephaly","Hydrocephaly","Macrocephaly"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Craniosynostosis is premature fusion of one or more cranial sutures, causing characteristic skull-shape distortion and sometimes raised intracranial pressure or developmental effects in multisuture disease.","more":"Craniosynostosis is premature fusion of one or more cranial sutures, causing characteristic skull-shape distortion and sometimes raised intracranial pressure or developmental effects in multisuture disease.\n\nCurriculum alignment: CNS 2024, p.25 (definition, types and investigation of craniosynostosis).\n\nHigh-yield point: Early recognition matters because suture fusion may restrict skull growth perpendicular to the fused suture.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q63 — Premature fusion of cranial sutures","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 10 · صفحة مطبوعة 7 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 63.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q87","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"First Permanent tooth to err up tis","o":["1 st molar","1 stincisor","1 st pre molar","2 nd pre molar"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The first permanent tooth to erupt is usually the first permanent molar at approximately six years. This is a near-duplicate of Q39, with a different option set; both source entries are retained.","more":"The first permanent tooth to erupt is usually the first permanent molar at approximately six years. This is a near-duplicate of Q39, with a different option set; both source entries are retained.\n\nCurriculum alignment: GIT 2024–2025 and GIT 2025, p.5.\n\nHigh-yield point: This item duplicates the concept of Q39, not the identical wording/options; preserve both source records for auditability.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q87 — First permanent tooth to erupt (repeat item)","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 11 · صفحة مطبوعة 8 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 87.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q88","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Developmental","q":"8 monthsoldchildwillbeabletodo","o":["Pincergrasp","St and with support","Sittingstably","Saysmama, papa"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"By around eight months, many infants can sit steadily without support; timing varies. The supplied developmental notes place unsupported sitting around 8–9 months and the red-flag threshold at nine months.","more":"By around eight months, many infants can sit steadily without support; timing varies. The supplied developmental notes place unsupported sitting around 8–9 months and the red-flag threshold at nine months.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Developmental milestones summary, pp.2–3; Developmental Assessment—red flags, p.2; Developmental-assessment, p.3.\n\nHigh-yield point: Sitting with support precedes stable unsupported sitting; do not confuse the two milestones.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q88 — Ability expected at eight months","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 11 · صفحة مطبوعة 8 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 88.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q94","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Developmental","q":"Childsmilesatfamiliarpersonsattheageof","o":["1 month","4 months","2 months","6 months"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"A social smile typically appears at around six to eight weeks (about two months).","more":"A social smile typically appears at around six to eight weeks (about two months).\n\nCurriculum alignment: Developmental milestones summary, p.1 (social smile); Developmental Assessment—red flags, p.1 (early social milestones).\n\nHigh-yield point: A social smile is a social interaction milestone, unlike a reflexive or spontaneous smile.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q94 — Age of social smile","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 11 · صفحة مطبوعة 8 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 94.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q96","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Delayed speech in a5 year old child with normal motorandadoptivedevelopmentismostlikelydueto","o":["Mental retardation","Kernicterus","Cerebral Palsy","Deafness"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"If a five-year-old has isolated speech/language delay with otherwise apparently normal motor and adaptive development, hearing impairment must be actively considered. Formal hearing assessment is essential; language disorders, autism-spectrum conditions and environmental factors are also part of a full evaluation.","more":"If a five-year-old has isolated speech/language delay with otherwise apparently normal motor and adaptive development, hearing impairment must be actively considered. Formal hearing assessment is essential; language disorders, autism-spectrum conditions and environmental factors are also part of a full evaluation.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Developmental Assessment—red flags, pp.1–2 (hearing, speech and language domains); Developmental-assessment, p.2.\n\nHigh-yield point: Always assess hearing in a child with speech delay, even when the child appears to respond to some sounds.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q96 — Delayed speech with otherwise normal motor/adaptive development","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 12 · صفحة مطبوعة 9 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 96.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q99","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Developmental","q":"An infants its with support by.......","o":["4 months","5 months","8 months","7 months"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Supported sitting develops during the middle of the first year. Different teaching charts place it at about five to six months; the supplied notes give six months with support, while some milestones references allow five months. The source-key choice of five months is within expected variation.","more":"Supported sitting develops during the middle of the first year. Different teaching charts place it at about five to six months; the supplied notes give six months with support, while some milestones references allow five months. The source-key choice of five months is within expected variation.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Developmental-assessment, p.3 (sitting with support at about six months); Developmental milestones summary, p.2.\n\nHigh-yield point: Supported sitting, tripod sitting and unsupported sitting are different milestones; unsupported sitting is expected later, around 8–9 months.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q99 — Age at which an infant sits with support","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 12 · صفحة مطبوعة 9 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 99.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q100","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Prevention","q":"A preventable cause of the mental retardation is","o":["Down's","Cretinism","Turner syndrome","Cerebral palsy"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Untreated congenital hypothyroidism can cause preventable severe neurodevelopmental impairment; early newborn screening and prompt levothyroxine treatment greatly reduce this risk. The older term “cretinism” is obsolete and should be replaced by congenital hypothyroidism with neurodevelopmental impairment.","more":"Untreated congenital hypothyroidism can cause preventable severe neurodevelopmental impairment; early newborn screening and prompt levothyroxine treatment greatly reduce this risk. The older term “cretinism” is obsolete and should be replaced by congenital hypothyroidism with neurodevelopmental impairment.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Endocrine 2025, pp.9–10 (congenital hypothyroidism and preventable intellectual disability if treated early).\n\nHigh-yield point: Congenital hypothyroidism is a time-critical, treatable cause; early detection prevents most severe neurologic sequelae.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q100 — Preventable cause of intellectual disability","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 12 · صفحة مطبوعة 9 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 100.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q109","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Bone age is normal in","o":["Genetic Short Stature","Hypothyroidism","Hypopituitarism","Emotional deprivation"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Familial/genetic short stature generally has a bone age close to chronological age and a growth pattern consistent with family potential. Endocrine causes such as hypothyroidism and GH deficiency more often delay bone age; psychosocial deprivation can also delay growth/bone maturation.","more":"Familial/genetic short stature generally has a bone age close to chronological age and a growth pattern consistent with family potential. Endocrine causes such as hypothyroidism and GH deficiency more often delay bone age; psychosocial deprivation can also delay growth/bone maturation.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Endocrine 2025, pp.7 and 47 (bone age in short-stature evaluation; causes of growth failure).\n\nHigh-yield point: Compare height velocity, parental heights, pubertal stage and bone age before labeling short stature as familial.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q109 — Condition in which bone age may be normal","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 13 · صفحة مطبوعة 10 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 109.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q112","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Increase in length in the first year of life","o":["12 cm","15 cm","25 cm","35 cm"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"A typical infant grows about 25 cm in length during the first year, from approximately 50 cm at birth to 75 cm at one year.","more":"A typical infant grows about 25 cm in length during the first year, from approximately 50 cm at birth to 75 cm at one year.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.5 (birth length around 50 cm and one-year length around 75 cm).\n\nHigh-yield point: Length velocity is fastest in infancy and slows after the first year.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q112 — Increase in length during the first year","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 13 · صفحة مطبوعة 10 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 112.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q115","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Number of dec i dio us teeth is","o":["20","24","28","32"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The complete primary (deciduous) dentition contains 20 teeth: 10 in each jaw.","more":"The complete primary (deciduous) dentition contains 20 teeth: 10 in each jaw.\n\nCurriculum alignment: GIT 2024–2025 and GIT 2025, p.4 (primary tooth eruption sequence and dentition).\n\nHigh-yield point: Primary dentition is generally complete by approximately 2.5–3 years.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q115 — Number of deciduous teeth","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 13 · صفحة مطبوعة 10 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 115.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q119","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Sitting height is equal to","o":["Head circumference","Chest circumference","Upper segment","Crown-heel length"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Sitting height is used as the upper body segment in standard anthropometric assessment; it is measured from crown to sitting surface/pubic symphysis according to the method used.","more":"Sitting height is used as the upper body segment in standard anthropometric assessment; it is measured from crown to sitting surface/pubic symphysis according to the method used.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Endocrine 2025, p.44 (upper segment equals sitting height).\n\nHigh-yield point: Upper segment = total height − lower segment in the course method.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q119 — Definition of sitting height","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 14 · صفحة مطبوعة 11 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 119.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q121","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Developmental","q":"Order of development of secondary sexual characters in male","o":["Testicular development→Pubic hair→Axillary→ hair","Pubic hair→Testicular development→Axillary hair→Beard","Testicular development→Beard→Public hair→ Axillary hair","Axillary hair→Beard→Public hair→Testicular development"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"In boys, testicular enlargement is the first reliable sign of gonadarche (Tanner genital stage 2). Pubic hair typically develops after or around the same broad phase, while axillary/facial hair generally appears later.","more":"In boys, testicular enlargement is the first reliable sign of gonadarche (Tanner genital stage 2). Pubic hair typically develops after or around the same broad phase, while axillary/facial hair generally appears later.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Endocrine 2025, p.49 (male puberty: testicular enlargement first; pubic hair and axillary hair later).\n\nHigh-yield point: Testicular volume of about 4 mL or greater is a common clinical marker of onset of male puberty.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q121 — Order of male secondary sexual development","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 14 · صفحة مطبوعة 11 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 121.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q124","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Birth length doubles at the age of","o":["1 year","2 years","3 years","4 years"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"A birth length of around 50 cm reaches around 100 cm by age four; this is a standard approximate growth rule.","more":"A birth length of around 50 cm reaches around 100 cm by age four; this is a standard approximate growth rule.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.5.\n\nHigh-yield point: At one year, length is around 75 cm; at four years, around 100 cm.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q124 — Age when birth length doubles","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 15 · صفحة مطبوعة 12 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 124.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q49","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Thebirthheightwilltripleby","o":["12 years","10 years","8 years","14 years"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The source key indicates approximately 12 years. This is an approximate teaching rule, less precise than direct height-velocity assessment and not a fixed biological threshold.","more":"The source key indicates approximately 12 years. This is an approximate teaching rule, less precise than direct height-velocity assessment and not a fixed biological threshold.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.5 (growth reference heights); Endocrine 2025, p.7 (growth assessment).\n\nHigh-yield point: The “triples by 12 years” rule is a rough mnemonic, not a diagnostic cutoff.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q49 — Age when birth height triples","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 8 · صفحة مطبوعة 5 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 49.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q55","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Upper segment and lower segment ratio is1.7 at birth, the condition where the ratio is more than normal","o":["Hyperthyroidism","Mucopolysachoses","shortlimbeddwarfism","Goiter"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"A relatively high upper-to-lower segment ratio indicates a relatively short lower segment. Disproportionate short-limb skeletal dysplasia can therefore increase the ratio. The source phrase “1.7 at birth” should not be treated as a universal diagnostic cutoff.","more":"A relatively high upper-to-lower segment ratio indicates a relatively short lower segment. Disproportionate short-limb skeletal dysplasia can therefore increase the ratio. The source phrase “1.7 at birth” should not be treated as a universal diagnostic cutoff.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Endocrine 2025, p.44 (age-specific U/L ratio); p.48 (syndrome associations and growth patterns).\n\nHigh-yield point: A high U/L ratio suggests relatively short legs; a low ratio suggests relatively long legs.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q55 — Increased upper/lower segment ratio in short-limbed dwarfism","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 9 · صفحة مطبوعة 6 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 55.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q64","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Chest circumference is measured a long","o":["Xi ph oid in mid expiration","Nipple in mid expiration","Xi ph oid in mid inspiration","Nipple in mid inspiration"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Chest circumference is generally measured at the nipple line using a tape perpendicular to the chest. Some measurement protocols specify a particular respiratory phase (mid-inspiration, mid-expiration, or quiet mid-respiration), so the exact phase is technique-dependent. The source key selects nipple line in mid-inspiration; treat that as the source’s intended protocol rather than a universal standard.","more":"Chest circumference is generally measured at the nipple line using a tape perpendicular to the chest. Some measurement protocols specify a particular respiratory phase (mid-inspiration, mid-expiration, or quiet mid-respiration), so the exact phase is technique-dependent. The source key selects nipple line in mid-inspiration; treat that as the source’s intended protocol rather than a universal standard.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.4 (anthropometric measurements); the precise respiratory-phase wording is not clearly specified in the supplied curriculum.\n\nHigh-yield point: When serially measuring, use the same anatomical level and respiratory phase each time.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q64 — Chest circumference measurement site and respiratory phase","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 10 · صفحة مطبوعة 7 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 64.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q90","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"A child with the mental age of7 and chronological age of7 years has an IQ of","o":["100","120","120","80"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The historical ratio-IQ formula is mental age divided by chronological age multiplied by 100. Here 7/7 × 100 = 100. This formula is historically taught and should not be used as the sole modern method of intellectual-disability diagnosis.","more":"The historical ratio-IQ formula is mental age divided by chronological age multiplied by 100. Here 7/7 × 100 = 100. This formula is historically taught and should not be used as the sole modern method of intellectual-disability diagnosis.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Developmental-assessment, p.2 (developmental assessment and school performance); no explicit IQ formula located in the supplied curriculum.\n\nHigh-yield point: Modern assessment considers adaptive functioning and standardized testing; ratio IQ is a historical educational formula.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q90 — IQ calculation using mental and chronological age","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 11 · صفحة مطبوعة 8 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 90.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q102","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"A child with the IQ50-70 isgradedas","o":["Mild mental retardation","Moderate mental retardation","Moderatelyintelligent","Severe mental retardation"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Traditional classifications used IQ around 50–70 for mild intellectual disability. Contemporary diagnosis does not rely on IQ alone; adaptive functioning and developmental history are essential, and severity is not assigned solely by a numerical band.","more":"Traditional classifications used IQ around 50–70 for mild intellectual disability. Contemporary diagnosis does not rely on IQ alone; adaptive functioning and developmental history are essential, and severity is not assigned solely by a numerical band.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Developmental-assessment, p.2 (developmental assessment; no explicit severity/IQ table clearly located in curriculum).\n\nHigh-yield point: Use current terminology “intellectual disability”; the older phrase in the source stem is outdated and stigmatizing.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q102 — Historical IQ range for mild intellectual disability","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 12 · صفحة مطبوعة 9 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 102.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q104","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Prevention","q":"Thefollowingisincorrectaboutthumbsucking","o":["Can lead to malocclusion","Isasignofinsecurity","Isasourceofpleasure","Must be treated vigorously"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Thumb-sucking is common self-soothing behavior in infancy and early childhood. Persistent, intense sucking beyond the preschool years can contribute to malocclusion, but it should not automatically be treated vigorously or punitively; use reassurance and gentle behavioral approaches when intervention is needed.","more":"Thumb-sucking is common self-soothing behavior in infancy and early childhood. Persistent, intense sucking beyond the preschool years can contribute to malocclusion, but it should not automatically be treated vigorously or punitively; use reassurance and gentle behavioral approaches when intervention is needed.\n\nCurriculum alignment: No direct dedicated thumb-sucking lecture identified in the supplied curriculum; this is an extended pediatric behavioral/dental topic.\n\nHigh-yield point: Avoid punishment; assess duration, intensity, age and dental effects.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q104 — Thumb-sucking: identifying the incorrect statement","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 13 · صفحة مطبوعة 10 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 104.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q110","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Obesity in associated with","o":["Frolich's syndrome","Prader-Willi syndrome","Laurence-Moon-Biedl","All of the above"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Obesity can be associated with Prader–Willi syndrome, Bardet–Biedl syndrome (historically grouped with Laurence–Moon–Biedl terminology), and hypothalamic obesity syndromes such as Fröhlich syndrome. The source option’s historical nomenclature requires updating.","more":"Obesity can be associated with Prader–Willi syndrome, Bardet–Biedl syndrome (historically grouped with Laurence–Moon–Biedl terminology), and hypothalamic obesity syndromes such as Fröhlich syndrome. The source option’s historical nomenclature requires updating.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.2 (obesity in children); Endocrine 2025, pp.2 and 48 (syndromic growth/puberty associations).\n\nHigh-yield point: Consider syndromic obesity when obesity is accompanied by developmental delay, dysmorphism, hypogonadism, retinal or renal findings.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q110 — Obesity-associated syndromes","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 13 · صفحة مطبوعة 10 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 110.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q117","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Twocarpelbonesareradiologicallyin X-ray most of the children by the end of","o":["1 year","2 years","3 years","4 years"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Carpal ossification centres appear progressively and their number can be used in skeletal-age assessment. However, the exact age at which two are visible in “most children” varies by sex, reference standard and radiographic technique; this is an extended bone-age detail rather than a core course fact.","more":"Carpal ossification centres appear progressively and their number can be used in skeletal-age assessment. However, the exact age at which two are visible in “most children” varies by sex, reference standard and radiographic technique; this is an extended bone-age detail rather than a core course fact.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Endocrine 2025, pp.7 and 47 (bone age assessed on left hand/wrist radiograph).\n\nHigh-yield point: Bone age is a composite assessment of multiple ossification centres, not a count of two bones alone.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q117 — Appearance of two carpal ossification centres","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 14 · صفحة مطبوعة 11 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 117.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-growth-and-development-accepted-Q123","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Developmental","q":"Preference of use of one handedness is evident by","o":["6 months","1 year","2 years","3 years"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"A clear hand preference may begin to emerge around two years, but development varies and a stable preference often becomes clearer later. Marked early hand preference before about 18 months can be a warning sign for asymmetric motor impairment and should prompt assessment.","more":"A clear hand preference may begin to emerge around two years, but development varies and a stable preference often becomes clearer later. Marked early hand preference before about 18 months can be a warning sign for asymmetric motor impairment and should prompt assessment.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Developmental milestones summary, pp.1–5 (fine motor); supplied curriculum does not specify a definitive handedness age.\n\nHigh-yield point: Early strong hand dominance in an infant should not be assumed to be normal handedness.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q123 — Age when hand preference becomes evident","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم (قسم Growth and Development) · صفحة PDF المشار إليها داخل ملف MD: 15 · صفحة مطبوعة 12 · صفحة مفتاح الإجابة: 16 · رقم السؤال الأصلي: 123.","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Growth_and_Development_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-miscellaneous-topics-accepted-Q102","spec":"eb-growth","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Developmental / elimination disorders","q":"Eco per es is is","o":["Involuntary passage of the faeces","Involuntary passage of urine","Both","None"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Encopresis is recurrent passage of faeces in inappropriate places in a child beyond the developmental age at which bowel control is expected. It may be associated with constipation and overflow soiling, or less commonly occur without constipation.","more":"Encopresis is recurrent passage of faeces in inappropriate places in a child beyond the developmental age at which bowel control is expected. It may be associated with constipation and overflow soiling, or less commonly occur without constipation. **Why the others are wrong:** (a) Involuntary passage of faeces: correct. (b) Involuntary passage of urine: enuresis, not encopresis. (c) Both: incorrect. (d) None: incorrect.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Developmental Assessment and Developmental milestones; paediatric history/continence. **High-yield point / Review Notes:** Assess constipation, stool withholding, painful defecation, psychosocial factors, and red flags.\n\nHigh-yield point: Encopresis is recurrent passage of faeces in inappropriate places in a child beyond the developmental age at which bowel control is expected.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q102 — Developmental / elimination disorders","aoSource":"Question-bank MD p.161 · printed p.311 · answer-key PDF p.169 · original Q102","aoSystem":"النمو والتطور","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Miscellaneous_Topics_Accepted.md","_genhy":true}]);
